Here is a simple Java Servlet program using Cookies and Session with a form and a display page. It works with Apache Tomcat 9.

# 1. Folder structure

Create a folder named `CookieSessionApp` inside Tomcat's `webapps` folder.

```
CookieSessionApp/
│
├── index.html
└── WEB-INF/
    ├── web.xml
    └── classes/
        ├── CookieServlet.class
        └── SessionServlet.class
```

You will create the HTML and XML files manually. The `.class` files are generated by compiling the Java files.

## 2. Form code — `index.html`

The user enters their name and submits the form.

```
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
    <title>Cookie and Session</title>
</head>
<body>
    <h2>Enter Your Details</h2>

    <form action="session" method="post">
        Name: <input type="text" name="username" required>
        <br><br>

        <input type="submit" value="Submit">
    </form>
</body>
</html>
```

## 3. Session Servlet — `SessionServlet.java`

This stores the name in a session and displays it.

```
import java.io.*;
import javax.servlet.*;
import javax.servlet.http.*;

public class SessionServlet extends HttpServlet {

    protected void doPost(HttpServletRequest request,
                          HttpServletResponse response)
                          throws IOException {

        response.setContentType("text/html");
        PrintWriter out = response.getWriter();

        String name = request.getParameter("username");

        HttpSession session = request.getSession();
        session.setAttribute("username", name);

        out.println("<html><body>");
        out.println("<h2>Session Example</h2>");
        out.println("<h3>Welcome " + name + "</h3>");
        out.println("<p>Name stored in session.</p>");
        out.println("</body></html>");
    }
}
```

How it works:

- `getParameter()` gets the name from the form.
- `getSession()` creates or retrieves a session.
- `setAttribute()` stores the name in the session.
- The servlet displays the name.

## 4. Cookie Servlet — `CookieServlet.java`

This creates a cookie to store the name in the browser and displays it.

```
import java.io.*;
import javax.servlet.*;
import javax.servlet.http.*;

public class CookieServlet extends HttpServlet {

    protected void doPost(HttpServletRequest request,
                          HttpServletResponse response)
                          throws IOException {

        response.setContentType("text/html");
        PrintWriter out = response.getWriter();

        String name = request.getParameter("username");

        Cookie cookie = new Cookie("username", name);
        cookie.setMaxAge(60 * 60);

        response.addCookie(cookie);

        out.println("<html><body>");
        out.println("<h2>Cookie Example</h2>");
        out.println("<h3>Welcome " + name + "</h3>");
        out.println("<p>Name stored in a cookie.</p>");
        out.println("</body></html>");
    }
}
```

How it works:

- `new Cookie()` creates a cookie.
- `setMaxAge(60 * 60)` sets its lifetime to one hour.
- `addCookie()` sends the cookie to the browser.
- The servlet displays the submitted name.

## 5. Display both examples from the same form

To test cookies too, change the form in `index.html` to:

```
<form action="session" method="post">
    Name: <input type="text" name="username" required>
    <br><br>
    <button type="submit">Store in Session</button>
</form>

<br>

<form action="cookie" method="post">
    Name: <input type="text" name="username" required>
    <br><br>
    <button type="submit">Store in Cookie</button>
</form>
```

Now the user can test either method separately.

## 6. Configure `web.xml`

Save this file inside `WEB-INF/web.xml`.

```
<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<web-app xmlns="http://xmlns.jcp.org/xml/ns/javaee"
         version="3.1">

    <servlet>
        <servlet-name>SessionServlet</servlet-name>
        <servlet-class>SessionServlet</servlet-class>
    </servlet>

    <servlet-mapping>
        <servlet-name>SessionServlet</servlet-name>
        <url-pattern>/session</url-pattern>
    </servlet-mapping>

    <servlet>
        <servlet-name>CookieServlet</servlet-name>
        <servlet-class>CookieServlet</servlet-class>
    </servlet>

    <servlet-mapping>
        <servlet-name>CookieServlet</servlet-name>
        <url-pattern>/cookie</url-pattern>
    </servlet-mapping>

</web-app>
```

## 7. Difference between Cookie and Session

| Cookie                                                          | Session                                              |
| --------------------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------- |
| Stored in the browser                                           | Data stored on the server                            |
| Created using `new Cookie()`                                    | Created using `request.getSession()`                 |
| Sent using `response.addCookie()`                               | Data stored using `session.setAttribute()`           |
| Can persist after the browser closes, depending on its lifetime | Usually expires after inactivity or when invalidated |
| Example: `cookie.setMaxAge(3600)`                               | Example: `session.invalidate()`                      |

## 8. How to run in Tomcat 9

1. Save `index.html` inside `CookieSessionApp`.
2. Save both `.java` files in a temporary source folder.
3. Compile them using the Tomcat 9 `lib/servlet-api.jar` file, placing the resulting `.class` files in `WEB-INF/classes`.
4. Ensure `web.xml` contains both servlet mappings.
5. Start Tomcat.
6. Open this URL in your browser:

`http://localhost:8080/CookieSessionApp/`

Enter a name and click Store in Session or Store in Cookie.

Expected output:

```
Session Example
Welcome Priya
Name stored in session.
```

For the cookie button, the output will be:

```
Cookie Example
Welcome Priya
Name stored in a cookie.
```

Remember for exams: A cookie stores data in the client browser, while a session stores user-specific data on the server. Both examples above display the submitted name.
